Well, to argue against my own point for a moment, there is a D&D
justification for it, the fact that alignment-changing equipment
usually carries, as a side-effect, a reluctance to remove the item as
part of the effect.  I'd suggest that reluctance be moved into the
effect of the item, as a special case in the take-off armor code
instead of autocursing.  That'd make it much harder to get rid of, but
certainly not impossible.


> I already responded a bit on this in my other post, but consider that if
> you make it harder to detect and remove curse, the consequences of
> putting on a cursed item become higher and higher, and people will
> simply become more and more cautious.

Only to a point.  Caution in Nethack will only take you so far.
Gaining experience is partly a false means of progress, for monster
difficulty increases as the player's experience rises.  In fact,
gaining experience rapidly is a good way to die because of the likes
of soldier ants and poison arrow users.  Equipment causes no
corresponding rise in monster difficulty, so the player has a strong
incentive to use such stuff as it appears.  If a death could have been
avoided by using an unknown item in inventory, then that's arguably a
death from bad decision-making.

Rogue was difficult so that the player had less room for caution.  If
some of Nethack's identification conveinences were scaled back, it
would force the player to become bolder in their use.

